            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Political Activism</strong><br>\nRussell was not just a philosopher; he was also a vocal political activist. He protested against World War I, for which he was imprisoned in 1918. His activism spanned issues from nuclear disarmament to advocating for social reform, reflecting his belief in humanitarian ideals and social justice.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Views on Religion and Society</strong><br>\nRussell was known for his criticism of organized religion, considering it a harmful social institution. He believed in individual liberty and rational analysis of societal problems. His controversial views were presented in his book \"Why I Am Not a Christian,\" which remains influential to this day.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Nobel Prize in Literature</strong><br>\nIn 1950, Russell was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature, an acknowledgment of his varied contributions to the field through his writings. The Nobel Committee recognized his humanitarian ideas and articulate defense of freedom of thought presented in his numerous works.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
