          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Russell's Philosophical Views</strong><br>\nRussell's philosophy was rooted in analytic tradition, emphasizing the importance of logical analysis and language in solving philosophical problems. He was a strong advocate for skepticism in philosophical inquiry, promoting a scientific approach to philosophy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Educational Contributions</strong><br>\nApart from his philosophical and mathematical works, Russell made significant contributions to education. He established an experimental school with his second wife, Dora Black, aimed at implementing progressive educational theories, which was a reflection of his innovative ideas in education.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nRussell's influence extends beyond philosophy and logic into the realms of education, politics, and social theory. His writings continue to be studied for their clarity and depth, and his work has influenced multiple fields, including the development of artificial intelligence and cognitive science. His legacy as a thinker who constantly sought knowledge and truth remains powerful and relevant.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
